  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component demonstrates strong foundational accessibility choices for its interactive controls (native buttons, correct icon hiding, proper external link handling, and visible focus indicators). However, it contains a critical structural gap in how the map canvas is exposed to assistive technology, and it fails to communicate dynamic state changes from the zoom/reset actions. These are not minor oversights; they break core access for screen reader users and leave keyboard users without confirmation of action success. The design requires revision before shipping.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reading the code, I predicted: (1) The map canvas would lack an accessible name/role, making it a black box to AT. (2) Zoom/reset actions would update state silently without `aria-live` or status announcements. (3) The \"text alternative\" link would be visually present but not programmatically associated with the map region. (4) Focus indicators and text contrast would pass. (5) Third-party map controls might lack keyboard support, making the custom buttons necessary but incomplete without focus management documentation. All predictions were confirmed during investigation.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Map canvas lacks accessible name and role; text alternative link is not programmatically associated.** \n   - `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:14` (`<div className=\"map-canvas\" />`) renders as an empty region to assistive technology. The comment notes a peer table link, but it sits in a separate `<p>` without `aria-describedby`, `role=\"img\"`, or figure/figcaption association. Screen reader users will either skip the map entirely or hear \"empty region\".\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 1.1.1 Non-text Content; WAI-ARIA APG Map pattern (requires `role=\"img\"` + `aria-label`, or programmatic association with text alternative)\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Interactive watershed boundary map showing county lines and elevation zones.\"` to the canvas div. Programmatically associate the table link using `aria-describedby=\"map-alt-text\"` on the canvas, and wrap the link in `<span id=\"map-alt-text\">View watershed boundaries as a data table</span>`, or use `<figure>`/`<figcaption>` semantics.\n\n2. **Dynamic zoom/pan/reset state changes are not communicated to assistive technology.**\n   - `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:4-6` handlers only log to console and presumably mutate third-party map state. No `aria-live=\"polite\"` region, `role=\"status\"`, or programmatic announcement exists. Screen reader users will press the button, hear nothing, and not know if the action succeeded or failed.\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users, keyboard-only users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages; WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern (state changes must be programmatically determinable)\n   - **Fix**: Add a visually hidden `<div aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"visually-hidden\" id=\"map-status\" />` and update its text content on each zoom/reset action (e.g., `Map zoomed in to level 12`, `View reset to default`). Ensure the third-party map library exposes a callback or use a MutationObserver if it updates DOM directly.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Missing keyboard support documentation and focus management for third-party map controls.**\n   - `WatershedMapPanel.jsx:23-35` provides custom buttons, but the comment states a third-party library mounts its canvas. If that library does not expose its own keyboard-accessible pan/zoom controls, keyboard users are forced to rely on these buttons. However, there is no focus management design: after clicking a button, where does focus go? Does it return to the trigger? What happens if the map steals focus during async tile loading?\n   - **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard; WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order\n   - **Fix**: Document keyboard support requirements for the third-party library. If it lacks them, implement a focus-safe pattern: ensure buttons don't shift focus on click, and if the map updates dynamically, use `aria-busy=\"true\"` during tile loads and restore focus to the last active control after completion.\n\n2. **Focus indicator contrast calculation relies on perimeter change but doesn't verify against adjacent interactive elements.**\n   - `styles.css:38` sets `outline: 3px solid #0b5fff; outline-offset: 2px;`. While the blue-to-white contrast (~9.3:1) passes WCAG 2.4.13's 3:1 requirement, the `outline-offset` creates a gap between the button border and the focus ring. If adjacent buttons are close (gap is 12px), the rings may visually merge or create confusion about which element is focused.\n   - **User group impacted**: Low vision users, cognitive accessibility users\n   - **WCAG/APG citation**: WCAG 2.4.13 Focus Appearance\n   - **Fix**: Verify focus rings don't overlap adjacent controls at standard zoom levels. Consider `outline-offset: 0` or reducing the gap to ensure clear visual separation without merging. Test at 200% zoom.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- The external link disclosure `(opens in a new tab)` is correctly implemented and meets WCAG 4.1.2 expectations. Note: some screen readers announce `target=\"_blank\"` natively, making the visually hidden text slightly redundant but harmless. Acceptable as-is.\n- Button padding (`6px 14px`) yields ~38x24 CSS pixels at standard font sizes, meeting WCAG 2.5.8 minimum target size (24x24). No issue.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Consider using `<figure>` and `<figcaption>` to semantically group the map canvas with its text alternative and controls. This improves document outline structure for screen reader users navigating by landmarks/headings. (`WatershedMapPanel.jsx:13-21`)\n- Add `aria-describedby` to the control buttons if they need contextual help (e.g., \"Adjusts map zoom level\"). Not required now but useful for complex maps.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Missing programmatic association between map and text alternative**: The table link is a peer route, not a fallback, but WCAG 1.1.1 requires the alternative to be programmatically linked to the non-text content so AT can present them together.\n- **Missing `aria-live` or status region for dynamic updates**: Zoom/pan/reset mutate state silently. No announcement mechanism exists.\n- **Missing keyboard operability contract for third-party map**: The component assumes custom buttons suffice, but doesn't address focus trapping, busy states during tile loading, or whether the library itself supports arrow keys/home/end.\n- **Missing error/failure state communication**: If the map fails to load or zooming throws an error, there's no `aria-live=\"assertive\"` region or error association pattern in place.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Map canvas is a black box; state changes silent; text alternative not associated |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Custom buttons work, but third-party map controls may lack keyboard support; focus behavior undefined after async updates |\n| Low vision | LOW | Focus indicators pass contrast and sizing; layout reflows normally at 200% zoom |\n| Cognitive | LOW | Labels are explicit; new-tab disclosure is clear; interaction model is consistent |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or auto-playing content present |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No audio/video elements in component |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Text/button contrast passes; no color-only state indicators |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component passes automated checks for button semantics, link structure, and focus visibility, which explains the developer's confidence. However, it fails at the foundational layer: the map canvas is inaccessible to assistive technology, and dynamic state changes are invisible to screen reader users. These are not preference issues; they block core functionality for AT users. The design requires structural fixes (map role/label, status announcements, programmatic association) before testing or shipping. Upgrading to ACCEPT requires resolving the CRITICAL and MAJOR findings above.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the third-party map library expose a keyboard-accessible API, or are these custom buttons the sole interaction method? If the latter, does it support arrow key panning or only click/scroll?\n- How does the component handle map loading failures or network timeouts? Is there an error state communicated to AT?\n- Are there multiple instances of this component on a page? If so, are `aria-describedby` IDs and `aria-live` regions scoped uniquely to avoid cross-instance announcement collisions?\n\n**Confidence Calibration**: \n- Map canvas/association finding: HIGH confidence (direct DOM inspection confirms missing role/label and unlinked link)\n- State communication finding: HIGH confidence (handlers only log; no live region or status pattern present)\n- Focus indicator merge risk: MEDIUM confidence (depends on exact zoom level and adjacent button spacing; requires visual verification)\n- Third-party keyboard support: MEDIUM confidence (assumed missing based on custom button reliance; requires library documentation review)",
